Subject: Report each data member's own C++ facts through `ParseCallbacks`

The Rust rendering of a struct drops things C++ said about its data members,
and two of the omissions decide whether the class has an implicitly declared
default constructor.

The first is the default member initializer. `struct S { int x = 5; };` and
`struct S { int x; };` generate the same Rust, and C++ tells them apart: an
initializer is what gives a `const` member its one and only value, what binds
a reference member, and what stands in for a member type's missing default
constructor. A generator which cannot see one either offers a constructor C++
has deleted or withholds one C++ declares.

The second is a bitfield's own type. A run of bitfields shares one allocation
unit and each member survives only as accessors over it, so `const int a : 3`
leaves no field for `--use-const-newtype-wrapper`'s marker to land on; the
marker reaches the accessors' signatures and nothing else.

`denote_data_member` reports both, for each member bindgen laid out, whether
or not a field of the member's own type is generated for it. Purely additive:
no generated code changes.

Finding the initializer is the interesting half. libclang has no query for one
and the child cursors do not answer it: they carry expressions belonging to
the *type* as well - an array bound - and clang's cursor visitor stops at a
bitfield's width expression without ever visiting the initializer which may
follow it, so a bitfield's is not among the children at all.
`Cursor::has_default_member_initializer` reads the declaration's tokens
instead: it scans from past the declarator-id - past the width expression too,
for a bitfield - and takes a `=` outside any bracket as an initializer, since
nothing in a declarator spells one there. A `{` counts only while everything
passed at bracket depth zero has been part of the declarator, which is what
tells it from the brace of a braced template argument in a trailing return
type. It answers `None` for a member declared by a macro expansion, whose
token range spans the macro's definition as well as its use and so is not the
field's own to read.

